A||||1||fierodave||Fastback trunk leaks & fabric stain removal||||2||
Z||000000||fierodave||12-16-2001||06:16 AM||||<BR>All fastback GT's have the plastic trunk square off parts and that will cause 90% of trunk leaks!<P>You hardly ever hear of leaks in a coupes right!  <P>almost Every 86-88 GT I have had that leaked in the trunk was caused by these plastic parts either haveing a crack in them or they needed to be resealed. Only Had one GT with a wing stud leak! <P>The plastic squre off parts are held in with one little screw and plastic bracket and have a foam gasket type material between them and the metal trunk part they sit on. they can get dirty and dried out and can leak, or the plactic square off's have a crack from people doing work on the engine <P>They may lean on the gasket on each side of the trunk and the plastic gets a crack! <P>No water will get in your trunk from the weep holes in the trunk bottom unless you drive in water high enough to put the bottom of your trunk under water!   <P>I was told by a tech at pontiac the holes in the bottom of the trunk are to prevent a condensation build up in the trunk, or if for some reason it leaked (square off's, wing,left open in rain!!!) :~)  <P>lay upside down in the trunk and run water along the edge of the gasket and the plastic part and watch where it's leaking in the trunk. get a good light supply in there with you, and you will find it!  <P>I had a GT that didn't leak at all, I installed a new gasket because the old one had that famous front section rip and the car was being restored and I wanted the new gasket for looks.  after the new gasket was put in  it leaked like heck. it took me three days to find the darn leak.  <BR>The leak was caused when I removed the old gasket!  Parts of the old rubber gasket are glued to the plastic square offs and I cracked one side when I took out the old gasket!  <P>I stood on my head in the trunk with a water hose in hand and found the leak in five minutes.   <P>It's hard to see the leak, it usually will leak on the most rear section of the car and drip down behind the carpet! <P>I Removed that square off, plastic welded it back up, and NO more leaks!<P>I look for leaks with the trunk <BR>carpet out of the car, It's a good time to power wash the trunk carpet anyway and clean it up! <P>Stains:<BR>BTW~~ Use WD-40 on carpet stains for oil grease & rust!  You will be surprized at how good it works on carpets, seats and headliners that get the black butyl tape residue on them.<BR>My  2¢ worth.<P>FD||||reg||2
Z||000001||terryk||12-16-2001||05:50 PM||||You might want to use a battery powered light source.....<P>I just wandered out to my old carpet and hit it with WD-40 in the really bad area's. That's amazing! It looks like new (once I washed out the WD-40.) I have to remember this one!||||reg||14
